<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#zb-vz-26">Glossary</a> · Term · weapon</p><h1 class="enc-title">ZB vz. 26</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The ZB vz. 26 was a light machine gun designed by Václav Holek at the Zbrojovka <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> arms works and adopted by the Czechoslovak army in 1926. Reliable and easy to maintain, it was exported widely and used by many armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Its design was the basis of the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bren-light-machine-gun">Bren gun</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zb-vz-26.jpg" alt="ZB vz. 26" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vitaly V. Kuzmin · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZB_vz._26_at_Great_Patriotic_War_museum_in_Smolensk.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Around 1921 the military of the young Czechoslovak state began looking for a light machine gun of its own. Early trials included foreign designs such as the Berthier, the M1918 Browning automatic rifle and the Madsen, and several domestic designs, of which the most important was the Praha II, a lightweight belt-fed weapon built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>.</p><p>Development of the ZB-26 began in 1923, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> arms factory was built, and production was moved to Zbrojovka Brno, which led to long court cases over royalties. Václav Holek was charged by the army with producing the gun, assisted by his brother Emmanuel and an Austrian and a Polish engineer. The brothers replaced the belt feed with a top-feeding box magazine, and the resulting Praha I-23 was selected. Manufacture had begun by late 1926, and the gun became the standard light machine gun of the Czechoslovak Army by 1928.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The army marked it the LK vz. 26, from lehký kulomet, light machine gun, and vzor, model; ZB-26 is the factory designation. It is a gas-operated, air-cooled, selective-fire weapon with a finned, quick-detachable barrel, firing from an open bolt. The 20-round sheet-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> magazine sits on top, so the sights are offset to the left and spent cartridges are ejected downwards. It had a folding bipod and could also be fitted to a tripod, serving to some extent as a medium machine gun or in an anti-aircraft role. Its reputation rested on reliability, simple components, the quick-change barrel and ease of manufacture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> bought 45,132 guns between the wars, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> is believed to have made more than 120,000 between 1926 and 1939, most often in 7.92×57mm Mauser. With the improved ZB-30 it was exported to twenty-four countries, with large batches for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. According to Brno, 30,249 went to China between 1927 and 1939, and Chinese arsenals made it as the Type 26. After the occupation of Czechoslovakia the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a> adopted it as the MG 26(t).</p><p>The British Bren gun was a modified ZB vz. 26 licensed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and the Japanese Type 97 tank machine gun was designed from captured guns. The gun saw service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaco-war">Chaco War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-civil-war">Chinese Civil War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ZB vz. 26” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
